WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.150
. We've previously talked about the reversing
of the yield curve, where you will have a

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:10.299
longer term bond that yields a lower interest
rate than a shorter term bond.

00:00:10.299 --> 00:00:15.820
And as I explained to you before, hopefully
somewhat clearly, I always I'm never sure

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.240
if I'm actually explaining these things clearly
enough.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.690
Typically, if you have the option of locking
up money for three years versus five years,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.380
you would expect to earn more interest when
you lock your money up for five years because

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.120
you are giving up access to the money for
a longer period of time.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.440
In exchange, you would expect to earn more
of an interest rate.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.660
And when the yield curve reverses, what it
means is that the longer bond actually yields

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.910
a lower rate and it's a known sign of uncertainty
and a negative outlook for the longer future

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:49.910
of the economy.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.860
If you're looking if you think the economy
is going to be a disaster over a five year

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.640
term, you may be willing to accept a lower
yield to tie up the money for five years,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.559
because you expect that during that five year
period it may get even worse and you want

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:06.729
to avoid a potentially even lower return.

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:09.790
That's the sort of idea of a yield curve reversal.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:15.370
We now have a different story, which is that
yesterday briefly and the numbers keep fluctuating.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.740
The one in three month Treasury bills had
yields go negative.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.000
OK, so understand what this means with treasury
bills and bonds, prices and yields move in

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.119
opposite directions.

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:31.950
So if you've got now we're talking nominally
magine a hundred dollar par value bill with

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.600
a 2 percent nominal interest rate.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:40.250
If I buy that bill for 100 bucks and I get
$2 for each year that I hold it, my return

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.250
is 2 percent.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.290
And then when I'm done, I get my hundred dollars
back for as long as I've held it, I've made

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.890
2 percent.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:47.890
Great.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.850
Imagine that the demand for that hundred dollar
bond goes up.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.420
People want to buy that hundred dollar bond.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:01.700
So even though it's one hundred dollar bond
or bill, you might pay $110 for it.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.830
Meaning you pay 110.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:08.899
You still get your 2 percent off of the hundred,
meaning your 2 bucks a year.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:12.260
And then when you give the bond back, you
only get one hundred dollars back.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.080
Remember, you paid 110.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.010
You get 100 back because that's the value
the face value of the bill.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:21.760
And you got your two bucks, your interest
rate, your your yield has now gone down.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.270
And you have to, of course, account for the
$10 that you lost.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.620
But then you made two bucks a year.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.160
You can also add inflation in there and you
find out your real return.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.300
When someone says imagine that the demand
went up like crazy and you bought a hundred

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:43.140
dollar bond for 200 bucks, it still pays 2
percent of the hundred dollars.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.340
You're getting your two dollars a year.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.349
But when you sell that bought, when you give
that bond back and you get the money back

00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:55.150
out of it, it's a hundred dollar bond that
two percent may not have actually made up

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.209
enough.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:59.050
You may be losing money in the net.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.140
It would be an effective negative yield on
that bond.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.530
Now, that's the concept of how yields can
be negative or positive.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.810
Now, what we're talking about now is something
a little bit different.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:14.830
Yesterday, the one month T-bill had a yield
of negative pointto 1 percent and the 3 month

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.489
had a yield of negative point 0 3 percent.

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:18.710
So how is that possible?

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:25.200
The math is that the yields can become negative
if the sale price of the bond goes super high.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.170
Meaning people are desperate, desperate, desperate
to buy bonds, even accounting for the interest

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:31.170
rate.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.080
You could be looking at losing money on that
bond.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.659
You will lose money on the bond.

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:38.659
And what happened is that the Fed slashed
rates to zero.

00:03:38.659 --> 00:03:40.760
So you're starting with a zero interest rate.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.040
And there is such demand for a safe haven
for money that people are willing to pay to

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.260
have their money held in treasuries instead
of earning one percent for letting the government

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.560
hold your money, which is normally normally
the way it works is you give the government

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.420
money, you earn something for doing that.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:05.459
When times are so uncertain, you might be
willing to pay the government pointto 1 percent

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:06.629
to hold the money.

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:08.909
Why on earth would you do this?

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:17.059
If you believe the outlook is so grim, knowing
you won't lose more than 0.01 percent could

00:04:17.059 --> 00:04:22.089
be better when you're talking about large
amounts of money, that could be better than

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:26.740
being exposed to larger losses if you didn't
do this.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:32.250
One other thing you won't necessarily actually
lose money with negative yields.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:37.750
On paper, it's important to understand that
you could have deflation reach a point where

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:42.139
the cash payments from the bond are actually
worth the purchase.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:45.520
Once you account for inflation, this stuff
doesn't really matter.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.809
The takeaway is the most important thing is
that this is a signal about the insane level

00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:54.960
of uncertainty that there is for the fourth.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:55.969
Seeable future.

00:04:55.969 --> 00:05:00.589
I think most of you probably understand that
that that's the most important bottom line.

00:05:00.589 --> 00:05:06.279
And what this is connected to, of course,
are corona virus cases, accelerating unemployment.

